---
import ParadoxLayout from '../layouts/ParadoxLayout.astro';
---

<ParadoxLayout slug="base-rate-neglect">
    <p class="text-slate-600 mb-6">
    	The Prosecutor’s Fallacy, or base-rate neglect, is the mistake of focusing on how strong or unusual
    	one piece of evidence seems while ignoring how common the underlying situation is to begin with. In
    	simple terms, people often confuse “this evidence would be unusual by chance” with “therefore this
    	explanation must be very likely"". See how this comes up on two scenarios: Cancer Screening Tests, and
    	a Courtroom DNA / Forensic Match.
    </p>

    <div class="bg-slate-50 p-5 rounded-xl border border-slate-100 mb-6">
        <label for="brn-scenario" class="block text-sm font-semibold text-slate-700 mb-2">Scenario:</label>
        <select id="brn-scenario" class="w-full md:w-auto px-4 py-2 rounded-lg border border-slate-300 bg-white text-slate-800 font-medium shadow-sm focus:outline-none focus:ring-2 focus:ring-purple-500 mb-6">
            <option value="cancer">Cancer Screening Test</option>
            <option value="dna">DNA / Forensic Match</option>
        </select>

        <p class="text-slate-600 mb-6" id="brn-intro-text">
						The Prosecutor’s Fallacy is the mistake of confusing the probability of seeing certain evidence
						if someone is innocent with the probability that they are innocent given that evidence. It ignores
						the base rate; how common guilt, innocence, or the matching evidence is in the larger population.
						This is the same basic error as base-rate neglect: people focus on the test’s accuracy and ignore
						how rare the disease is in the population being tested. So hearing that a cancer test is “93%
						accurate” can make people assume a positive result means a 93% chance of having cancer, when the
						true probability may be much lower if the cancer itself is uncommon and false positives occur.

        This is one of the most common reasoning errors in
        medicine and the courtroom: people hear "the test is 90% accurate" and assume a positive
        result means a 90% chance of being right. It usually doesn't. What matters just as much as
        the test's accuracy is how rare the thing it's testing for was to begin with &mdash; the
        <strong>base rate</strong>. Adjust the sliders below and watch the grid of 10,000 people
        change color.
        </p>

        <div class="grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-3">
            <div>
                <label class="flex justify-between text-sm font-semibold text-slate-700 mb-2">
                    <span id="brn-prior-label">Condition Prevalence</span>
                    <span id="brn-prior-val" class="text-purple-600 font-mono">1 in 100</span>
                </label>
                <!-- Log-scale slider (0-1000 maps internally to a probability between
                     1-in-10,000 and 1-in-2); see brnPriorFromSlider() in the script. -->
                <input type="range" id="brn-prior-slider" min="0" max="1000" step="1" value="541" class="w-full accent-purple-600">
            </div>
            <div id="brn-sensitivity-wrap">
                <label class="flex justify-between text-sm font-semibold text-slate-700 mb-2">
                    <span id="brn-sensitivity-label">Test Sensitivity</span>
                    <span id="brn-sensitivity-val" class="text-purple-600 font-mono">90%</span>
                </label>
                <!-- Value is hundredths of a percent (0-9999 = 0.00%-99.99%); range/default set per-scenario in JS. -->
                <input type="range" id="brn-sensitivity-slider" min="5000" max="9999" step="1" value="9000" class="w-full accent-purple-600">
            </div>
            <div>
                <label class="flex justify-between text-sm font-semibold text-slate-700 mb-2">
                    <span id="brn-specificity-label">Test Specificity</span>
                    <span id="brn-specificity-val" class="text-purple-600 font-mono">90%</span>
                </label>
                <!-- Value is hundredths of a percent (0-9999 = 0.00%-99.99%); range/default set per-scenario in JS. -->
                <input type="range" id="brn-specificity-slider" min="5000" max="9999" step="1" value="9000" class="w-full accent-purple-600">
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-3 items-start">
        <div class="md:col-span-2">
            <canvas id="brn-canvas" class="w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-xl bg-white" style="aspect-ratio: 1 / 1;"></canvas>
            <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-4 justify-center mt-4 text-sm">
                <div class="flex items-center gap-2"><span class="w-3 h-3 rounded-sm inline-block" style="background:#dc2626"></span>Positive &amp; actually has it</div>
                <div class="flex items-center gap-2"><span class="w-3 h-3 rounded-sm inline-block" style="background:#f59e0b"></span>Positive but doesn't have it</div>
                <div class="flex items-center gap-2"><span class="w-3 h-3 rounded-sm inline-block" style="background:#cbd5e1"></span>Negative &amp; correct</div>
                <div class="flex items-center gap-2"><span class="w-3 h-3 rounded-sm inline-block" style="background:#334155"></span>Negative but wrong</div>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div id="brn-result" class="bg-purple-50 text-purple-900 rounded-lg p-5 text-base leading-relaxed"></div>
    </div>

    <p class="text-xs text-slate-500 mt-6" id="brn-footnote">
        Further reading:
        <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Base_rate_fallacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-purple-600 underline hover:text-purple-800">Base Rate Fallacy on Wikipedia</a>
        &middot;
        <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prosecutor%27s_fallacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-purple-600 underline hover:text-purple-800">Prosecutor's Fallacy on Wikipedia</a>.
    </p>
</ParadoxLayout>

<script>
    import '../scripts/paradoxes/base-rate-neglect.js';
</script>
